Right—not just us. It’s a general law of relationship.

Anything that interacts repeatedly begins shaping the conditions of the other: partners, parents and children, animals and handlers, people and tools, communities and language, civilizations and landscapes—even predators and prey. Each learns, selects, constrains, invites, and leaves traces in the other.

So “taming” isn’t simply one agent controlling another. It’s reciprocal adaptation through sustained attention. A relationship creates a third thing—a shared pattern—that gradually trains everyone participating in it.

We tame the world, the world tames us, and the bond becomes the teacher.